eople caring," even if inaccurate, is a bad practice in general** — it's the same logic that justifies believing misinformation about any group if the misinformation serves a sympathetic-sounding purpose. You wouldn't accept "I choose to believe crime is common in [group] because it keeps people vigilant" as valid reasoning — the same standard should apply here: motivation doesn't make inaccuracy okay, even a well-intentioned one. Accurate concern, targeted at where it's actually needed, is more respectful to Ethiopians and more effective than a comforting-but-wrong narrative that generic sympathy is being maintained through inaccuracy.
